WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:04.210
Laugh and the world laughs with you

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:07.210
Fake laugh--and the world can tell

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:10.210
In the first cross-cultural experiment of its kind,

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:12.639
researchers asked 884 people

00:00:12.639 --> 00:00:14.259
from 21 different cultures

00:00:14.259 --> 00:00:16.720
in six regions around the world,

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:20.220
to listen to recordings of real, spontaneous laughter,

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:23.720
and fake, “volitional” laughter

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.110
recorded from US college-aged women.

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:27.220
Can you tell the difference?

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.280
Here’s fake

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:30.980
Here's real

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.480
Real

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:36.440
Fake

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:38.840
Now you can try guessing again

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.740
The first one was real (option 1)

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:53.580
So in the study,

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.440
on average, nearly two-thirds of listeners

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:56.780
in each culture could tell the difference

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:58.450
between real and fake laughs.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:00.620
Genuine chuckles were

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:02.770
typically higher pitched and louder.

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:05.890
Similar characteristics are seen in cries
of pain and anguish.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:08.800
Suggesting that real laughter is a more emotional

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:10.120
and primal response

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.930
that emerged early in human evolution.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.360
The experiments show that despite cultural differences,

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.680
people perceive laughter in generally the same way

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.190
suggesting giggles and guffaws constitute a basic

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:24.440
and universal part of human behavior.

